assistantfinal**Answer**

In the rotating (synodic) frame the equations of motion can be written as the gradient of the effective
potential  

\[
U(x,y)=-\frac{GM_{1}}{\rho _1}-\frac{GM_{2}}{\rho _2}-\frac{\omega ^2}{2}\,(x^{2}+y^{2}),
\qquad 
\rho _i=\sqrt{(x-x_i)^2+(y-y_i)^2}\;(i=1,2),
\]

plus the Coriolis terms.  Hence  

\[
\boxed{\;
\begin{aligned}
\ddot{x}-2\omega\dot y &=\frac{\partial U}{\partial x},\\[2mm]
\ddot{y}+2\omega\dot x &=\frac{\partial U}{\partial y}.
\end{aligned}
\;}
\]

Solving for the second‑time‑derivatives gives

\[
\boxed{\;
\begin{aligned}
\ddot{x}&=\frac{\partial U}{\partial x}+2\omega\dot y,\\[2mm]
\ddot{y}&=\frac{\partial U}{\partial y}-2\omega\dot x.
\end{aligned}
\;}
\]

---

### Evaluating the partial derivatives

Because  

\[
\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\Bigl(\frac{1}{\rho_i}\Bigr)
= -\frac{x-x_i}{\rho_i^{3}},\qquad
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\Bigl(\frac{1}{\rho_i}\Bigr)
= -\frac{y-y_i}{\rho_i^{3}},
\]

we obtain  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial U}{\partial x}
&= -GM_{1}\Bigl(-\frac{x-x_{1}}{\rho_1^{3}}\Bigr)
   -GM_{2}\Bigl(-\frac{x-x_{2}}{\rho_2^{3}}\Bigr)
   -\omega^{2}x                                                \\[2mm]
&= GM_{1}\frac{x-x_{1}}{\rho_1^{3}}
   +GM_{2}\frac{x-x_{2}}{\rho_2^{3}}
   -\omega^{2}x,                                               \\[4mm]
\frac{\partial U}{\partial y}
&= GM_{1}\frac{y-y_{1}}{\rho_1^{3}}
   +GM_{2}\frac{y-y_{2}}{\rho_2^{3}}
   -\omega^{2}y .
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### Final form of the equations of motion

Putting the gradient components back into the expressions for \(\ddot{x}\) and \(\ddot{y}\) we get

\[
\boxed{\;
\begin{aligned}
\ddot{x}&=
GM_{1}\frac{x-x_{1}}{\rho_1^{3}}+
GM_{2}\frac{x-x_{2}}{\rho_2^{3}}
-\omega^{2}x\;+\;2\omega\dot y,\\[4mm]
\ddot{y}&=
GM_{1}\frac{y-y_{1}}{\rho_1^{3}}+
GM_{2}\frac{y-y_{2}}{\rho_2^{3}}
-\omega^{2}y\;-\;2\omega\dot x .
\end{aligned}
\;}
\]

These two equations give the accelerations \(d^{2}x/dt^{2}\) and \(d^{2}y/dt^{2}\) expressed entirely in terms
of the effective potential \(U\) (through its spatial derivatives) together with the Coriolis terms
that arise in the rotating reference frame.
